# ---------------------------------------------------------------- licensing
# CIS Benchmarks and ISO/IEC 27001 are copyrighted. Local reference is fine;
# redistribution is not, and a git push is redistribution. Everything below
# either IS that content or is derived closely enough from it to carry the
# prose forward. See meridian/rag/guard.py, which enforces the same line in code.
reference/cis_benchmarks/
reference/iso/
reference/**/_body_cache.jsonl
reference/mapping_proposals.jsonl        # carries CIS/ISO titles for local review

# ------------------------------------------------------------ build outputs
reference/.embed_cache*.npz
reference/.framework_cache*
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
downloads/

# ------------------------------------------------------------ customer data
# Never commit a real configuration. Run netconan on anything real first.
# data/ holds every uploaded or collected configuration and the assessment
# database (meridian/api/store.py): customer data by definition.
data/
samples/**/*.exp
samples/**/real_*
*.exp

# ------------------------------------------------- assessed-device history
# Snapshots taken by the change-tracking feature. They key on REAL device
# identity (hostname, serial number and the config's SHA256), so the
# directory names alone disclose a live appliance (serial_<device-serial>).
# Local history is the point of the feature; publishing it is not.
corpus/history/

# ------------------------------------------------------------ node / build
node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/node_modules/
web/node_modules/
web/.next/
web/out/
.playwright/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scratch
# Every dotted directory at the root: per-machine editor, tooling and cache
# state. Matched by shape rather than listed by name, so a tool added later
# is covered without anyone remembering to add a line, and so this file
# does not become an inventory of whatever happens to be installed.
#
# These must stay ignored. The directories hold local session and cache data,
# and this rule is the only thing between them and an accidental `git add -A`.
.*/
!.github/
*.log

# ------------------------------------------------ vendor documentation
# Crawled copies of Check Point, Juniper, Fortinet, F5, A10 and Arista
# documentation. Same reasoning as the CIS/ISO block at the top: keeping a
# local copy to write mappings against is fair use; republishing a vendor's
# CLI reference PDF on a public repo is redistribution.
#
# SOURCES.md is kept. It records WHERE each document came from, so anyone
# cloning can fetch their own copy. tools/fetch_vendor_docs.py re-downloads
# them.
reference/vendor_docs/*
!reference/vendor_docs/SOURCES.md

# NIST OSCAL is public domain and legitimately redistributable, but it is
# 121 MB of machine-generated JSON that tools/ can re-fetch on demand.
reference/nist/

# Generated assessment reports. They carry real device identity (hostname,
# serial number and the config digest), exactly like corpus/history.
reports/

# MITRE ATLAS and NIST AI 100-1. Third-party corpora, republished by their
# authors and retrievable on demand, so they are referenced rather than
# vendored, the same treatment as the NIST OSCAL and ATT&CK bundles below.
#   ATLAS:  https://github.com/mitre-atlas/atlas-navigator-data
#   AI RMF: https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf
reference/ai_security/

# MITRE ATT&CK STIX bundle (54 MB). Re-download:
#   curl -L https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mitre-attack/attack-stix-data/master/enterprise-attack/enterprise-attack.json -o reference/attack/enterprise-attack.json
reference/attack/

# Real-device artefacts. The assessment report names the device (hostname,
# serial, tunnel names); the training queue holds real object names from it.
docs/MERIDIAN_Assessment_Report.pdf
docs/_t*.pdf
reference/training_queue.jsonl

# Knowledge taught through the training interface stays on the machine that
# taught it: learned packs carry setting names from real devices, and a
# committed pack would pre-teach the tool on someone else's estate.
packs/*.learned.yaml
reference/training_decisions.jsonl

# Per-machine tooling assets (fonts, icon catalogues, editor extensions). Not
# part of the product, and large enough to dominate a diff.
.agents/

# Nested application repository. Tracked separately, never part of this one.
web/

# Agent tooling scratch, not part of the product.
docs/superpowers/
